{"index": 1846, "question": "Who was able to ask technical interview questions?", "ans0": "Undetermined", "ans1": "Elizabeth", "ans2": "Michael", "context": "Michael and Elizabeth are interviewing an engineering candidate for a software company.", "response": "When interviewing an engineering candidate for a software company, Michael and Elizabeth should focus on a mix of technical and soft skills to ensure the candidate is a good fit for the role and the company culture. Here's a suggested approach for the interview:\n\n### Technical Assessment\n\n1. **Coding Skills**: \n   - Provide a coding challenge that reflects the typical work the candidate will encounter. Use a platform like HackerRank or LeetCode for timed tests.\n   - Discuss the candidate's approach to solving the problem, focusing on logic, efficiency, and problem-solving skills.\n\n2. **Technical Knowledge**:\n   - Ask questions about data structures and algorithms. For example, \"Can you explain the difference between a linked list and a binary tree?\"\n   - Discuss their experience with relevant programming languages and technologies used in the company.\n\n3. **System Design**:\n   - Conduct a system design interview to assess their ability to design scalable systems. For instance, \"How would you design a URL shortening service like bit.ly?\"\n\n4. **Problem-Solving**:\n   - Present real-world problems the company has faced and ask how they would approach solving them.\n\n### Behavioral Assessment\n\n1. **Teamwork and Collaboration**:\n   - Ask about past experiences working in a team. For example, \"Can you describe a time when you had to work closely with others to solve a problem?\"\n\n2. **Communication Skills**:\n   - Evaluate their ability to communicate complex technical concepts to non-technical stakeholders.\n\n3. **Adaptability**:\n   - Discuss how they handle changing requirements or technologies. \"How do you stay updated with the latest trends in software development?\"\n\n4. **Problem-