6. **Geometry and Trigonometry:**\n   - **Problem:** Solve problems involving geometric figures or trigonometric identities.\n   - **Strategy:** Visualize the problem, draw diagrams, and use known formulas. For trigonometry, remember identities and relationships.\n\n7. **Optimization:**\n   - **Problem:** Maximize or minimize a function subject to constraints.\n   - **Strategy:** Use calculus (e.g., Lagrange multipliers) or linear programming techniques. Understand the constraints and objective function clearly.\n\n**General Tips:**\n- **Practice Regularly:** Work on a variety of problems to build familiarity and confidence.\n- **Time Management:** Allocate time wisely and move on if stuck, returning to difficult problems later.\n- **Clear Communication:** Explain your thought process and reasoning clearly, as interviewers often value the approach as much as the solution.\n- **Stay Calm:** Maintain composure, as stress can hinder problem-solving ability.\n\nBy preparing with these strategies and practicing a wide range of problems, Daniel and Margaret can enhance their chances of success in the interview."}
